{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md && git status --short docs/design/dao.space.query.md docs/design/dao.stream.md && git diff --check -- docs/design/dao.space.query.md && git diff -- docs/design/dao.space.query.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md && git status --short docs/design/dao.space.query.md docs/design/dao.stream.md && git diff --check -- docs/design/dao.space.query.md && git diff -- docs/design/dao.space.query.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: code-review-pr\ndescription: >-\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\n---\n\n# Code Review PR Skill\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n  - problematic code\n  + suggested fix\n  ```\n\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\n\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\n\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Utility Script Reference\n\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\n\n---\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls\n\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n M docs/design/dao.space.query.md\n M docs/design/dao.stream.md\ndiff --git a/docs/design/dao.space.query.md b/docs/design/dao.space.query.md\nindex 878daa9..8d3b599 100644\n--- a/docs/design/dao.space.query.md\n+++ b/docs/design/dao.space.query.md\n@@ -1,9 +1,13 @@\n # dao.space.query — The Reader-Side Index Consumer\n \n-Status: implemented. The read coordinate is an explicit bounded DaoStream descriptor, interpretation enters only through explicit immutable\n-views (`current`, `history`) or explicit stream wrappers (`query/relation`, `query/entity-map-relation`), the index realization is owned\n-by `dao.space.index`, and `match` / `q` / `pull` run over one evaluator on\n-every platform, returning local closed bounded result DaoStreams. This\n+Status: implemented, on DaoStream v2. Every database input is a **value**:\n+a relation value (`query/relation`, `query/entity-map-relation`), a datom\n+view over one (`current`, `history`), or a published covered index the caller\n+opened (`open-published!`). `q` opens nothing and closes nothing. A live\n+DaoStream enters only through `snapshot`, the single interpreter over\n+`dao.stream.observe/step`. The index realization is owned by\n+`dao.space.index`, and `match` / `q` / `pull` run over one evaluator on every\n+platform, returning a local result value that `collect` materializes. This\n document records the read model, the source model, the index-realization\n decision, the query surface, and the open items. The executable contract is\n `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc`.\n@@ -23,25 +27,31 @@ decision, the query surface, and the open items. The executable contract is\n \n ## Architecture\n \n-The query library is an embeddable Peer over bounded DaoStreams. It opens each\n-explicit descriptor, consumes its logical tuples, and runs matching / Datalog /\n-pull above them. A published-index stream resolves its content-store coordinate\n-and manifest address below this boundary. The query library is pure\n-and stateless — it owns no durable state, never writes, and enforces no\n-schema; any schema policy belongs on the write side.\n+The query library is an embeddable Peer over finite relations of tuples. It\n+consumes the logical tuples of each value it is handed and runs matching /\n+Datalog / pull above them. An opened published index resolves its content-store\n+coordinate and manifest address below this boundary; the caller opens it and\n+the caller closes it. The query library is pure and stateless — it owns no\n+durable state, never writes, opens nothing, and enforces no schema; any schema\n+policy belongs on the write side.\n \n ```\n dao.space.query/q …/match …/pull         ← the TUPLE SPACE reads\n-     │  consumes exact-bounded logical tuple streams\n+     │  consumes finite relation values and opened indexes\n      ▼\n dao.jing content store (content handles) ← STORAGE boundary\n      ▲\n dao.space.index/publish-index!  (write side, a separate library)\n ```\n \n-## The read coordinate: bounded DaoStream descriptor\n+## The read coordinate: a serializable name, opened by the caller\n \n-A published covered index must be exposed as a bounded DaoStream descriptor/realization whose logical elements are canonical d5, with physical B-tree segments hidden below the adapter. The descriptor contains a resolvable content-store coordinate plus manifest address, not necessarily a live handle; its portability is conditional on that coordinate being resolvable by the receiving runtime.\n+A published covered index is named by a **serializable coordinate** whose\n+logical elements are canonical d5, with physical B-tree segments hidden below\n+it. The coordinate contains a resolvable content-store coordinate plus manifest\n+address, never a live handle; its portability is conditional on that coordinate\n+being resolvable by the receiving runtime. It is data, and may travel through a\n+stream like any other data.\n \n ```clojure\n (index/published-index\n@@ -49,7 +59,9 @@ A published covered index must be exposed as a bounded DaoStream descriptor/real\n    :path \"/srv/datom-world/content.log\"}\n   :segment/sha256-<manifest-hash>)\n \n-;; The resulting descriptor has this exact data shape:\n+;; The resulting coordinate has this exact data shape. The :dao.stream/*\n+;; keys are dao.space.index's. `open-published!` validates that exact\n+;; shape before opening anything; `q` dispatches on neither key.\n {:dao.stream/type :dao.space.index/published\n  :dao.stream/bound {:manifest-address :segment/sha256-<manifest-hash>}\n  :dao.stream/comparator :dao.space.index/eavt\n@@ -58,18 +70,19 @@ A published covered index must be exposed as a bounded DaoStream descriptor/real\n  :manifest-address :segment/sha256-<manifest-hash>}\n ```\n \n-A query may receive several descriptors as separate `:in` database values. A DaoStream descriptor is itself data and may be transported through another DaoStream.\n-Source identity is interpreter context and never a tuple slot. It is never\n-inferred from a DaoJing intake stream, and **a bare content-store handle\n-carries no source**: it must be wrapped in a valid DaoStream descriptor before\n-`q`/`match`/`pull` will read it, and passing one unwrapped throws.\n+A query may receive several opened indexes as separate `:in` database values.\n+The coordinate is itself data and may be transported through a stream, then\n+opened by whoever receives it. Source identity is interpreter context and never\n+a tuple slot. It is never inferred from a DaoJing intake stream, and **a bare\n+content-store handle carries no source**: it names no manifest, so\n+`q`/`match`/`pull` reject it like any other loose value.\n \n Two kinds of pools must not be confused:\n \n - **DaoJing intake pools** are physical ingestion topology — which streams an\n   observer materializes into content storage.\n-- **Query db-values** are semantic composition — which bounded streams a\n-  reader names independently in `:in`.\n+- **Query db-values** are semantic composition — which relation values and\n+  opened indexes a reader names independently in `:in`.\n \n They never need to coincide, and DaoJing never learns the latter. A manifest\n address is a content address like any other; it is a snapshot-at-read of an\n@@ -79,56 +92,109 @@ as such.\n ## Source polymorphism\n \n `dao.space.query/q` accepts database values only as:\n-1. A serializable exact-bounded DaoStream descriptor carrying `:dao.stream/type` and `:dao.stream/bound`\n-2. An already-opened closed fully-retained realization satisfying Reader+Bound.\n \n-Raw maps/vectors are not `q` database inputs; callers explicitly wrap arbitrary mixed-dimensional tuples with `query/relation` and entity maps with `query/entity-map-relation`.\n+1. A **relation value** — `(query/relation tuples)` or\n+   `(query/entity-map-relation maps)`.\n+2. A **datom view** over one — `(current src)` / `(history src)`, where `src`\n+   is a relation value, a `snapshot` result, or an opened published index.\n+3. An **opened published index** — `(query/open-published! coordinate)`.\n+\n+Raw maps and vectors are not `q` database inputs, and neither is anything else\n+loose: an unrecognized value throws before evaluation, naming what is accepted.\n+Callers explicitly wrap arbitrary mixed-dimensional tuples with\n+`query/relation` and entity maps with `query/entity-map-relation`.\n \n Each database input is one logical relation:\n \n-- **A published index** — a bounded DaoStream descriptor. `q` opens the\n-  descriptor and owns the realization for that query. A realization supplied\n-  directly by the caller is borrowed and is never closed by `q`.\n+- **A published index** — the caller opens the serializable coordinate with\n+  `open-published!`, holds the content-store handle, and releases it with\n+  `close-published!`. `q` borrows it and never closes it. There is no\n+  registry and no dispatch on a transport type: opening a coordinate is\n+  `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` plus a manifest read, a function call the caller\n+  makes, not a stream operation.\n - **An explicit datom view** — `current` and `history` are semantic view\n-  values interpreted by `q`/`match`/`pull`; their nested source remains the\n-  bounded DaoStream descriptor or realization. `(current source)` resolves a canonical d5 history and\n-  projects it to d3; `(history source)` preserves exact d5. Independent\n-  physical sources stay separate database inputs.\n-- **A wrapped relation** — `(query/relation [[...] ...])` wrapping arbitrary and mixed arities. Arity never selects an interpretation.\n-- **A wrapped entity-map collection** — `(query/entity-map-relation [{:db/id e, :work/status :todo, ...} ...])`. Normalized to datoms first: each `k v` pair becomes an `[e k v]` fact. Identity is explicit: the read side is pure and never mints an entity id, so every entity map must carry `:db/id`, and a map without one throws an informative `ex-info` rather than inventing a tempid.\n-\n-`q` consumes each input exactly as far as evaluation requires. It interprets\n-datom views, opens and closes descriptor-owned source realizations during\n-evaluation, and directly opens ordinary stream descriptors. A `current` view\n-(`as-of` nil) over a published covered index is realized **lazily**: the\n+  values interpreted by `q`/`match`/`pull`; their nested source is a relation\n+  value, a `snapshot` result (its relation is taken), or an opened published\n+  index.\n+  `(current source)` resolves a canonical d5 history and projects it to d3;\n+  `(history source)` preserves exact d5. Independent physical sources stay\n+  separate database inputs.\n+- **A wrapped relation** — `(query/relation [[...] ...])` wrapping arbitrary\n+  and mixed arities. Arity never selects an interpretation.\n+- **A wrapped entity-map collection** —\n+  `(query/entity-map-relation [{:db/id e, :work/status :todo, ...} ...])`.\n+  Normalized to datoms first: each `k v` pair becomes an `[e k v]` fact.\n+  Identity is explicit: the read side is pure and never mints an entity id, so\n+  every entity map must carry `:db/id`, and a map without one throws an\n+  informative `ex-info` rather than inventing a tempid.\n+\n+`q` consumes each input exactly as far as evaluation requires, and **opens and\n+closes nothing** — it is a pure function of its arguments. A `current` view\n+(`as-of` nil) over an opened published index is realized **lazily**: the\n covered sets are re-attached as restored B-trees and only the slices the plan\n-touches are fetched; every other input — history views, `as-of` bounds, raw\n-streams, in-process relations — is consumed eagerly before returning.\n-Already-opened realizations are borrowed and never closed. It performs\n+touches are fetched; every other input — history views, `as-of` bounds,\n+in-process relations — is consumed eagerly before returning. It performs\n arbitrary-dimensional exact positional matching/unification (with explicit `&`\n-tail syntax) and returns a local closed bounded result DaoStream.\n-`query/collect` materializes conventional Datalog shapes.\n-\n-The local result realization and derived realizations returned when a view is\n-applied to an already-open source are intentionally not DaoStream descriptors:\n-they are not serializable or reopenable transport identities. To transport a\n-result, first materialize it with `query/collect`, then wrap the retained tuples\n-explicitly with `query/relation`.\n+tail syntax) and returns a local **result value** carrying the find spec.\n+`query/collect` materializes conventional Datalog shapes from it.\n+\n+Relation values, view values and result values are values, not transports.\n+They carry no `:dao.stream/type` and no bound: a finite collection computed in\n+full has no second observer, no future appends and no eviction, so nothing\n+about the DaoStream reader surface would describe it (see *Decisions*). None of\n+them carries a reopenable transport identity — a result names nothing another\n+runtime could resolve. Their *contents*, being ordinary data, can of course\n+travel through a stream that carries data; to hand a result on that way,\n+materialize it with `query/collect` and wrap the retained tuples explicitly\n+with `query/relation`.\n \n Source polymorphism is an ergonomic property of the query *function*, not a\n-second medium — a local realization is by definition not shared, and\n-coordination between agents still runs through shared content storage.\n+second medium — a query value is not itself a shared medium, and coordination\n+between agents still runs through shared content storage.\n+\n+## Snapshots: the one stream interpreter\n+\n+Query never reads a live stream during evaluation. A DaoStream v2 reader\n+becomes a query input only by an explicit snapshot, and `snapshot` is the only\n+function in this namespace that requires `dao.stream`:\n+\n+```clojure\n+(query/snapshot handle)\n+;; => {:relation {:dao.space.query/relation [v …]}\n+;;     :status   :ended | :blocked | :gap | :defect\n+;;     :cursor   c            ; the cursor reached; on :gap the last retained\n+;;     :recovery c'           ; :gap only\n+;;     :read     raw}         ; :defect only\n+```\n+\n+It mints at `:dao.stream/oldest` and loops `dao.stream.observe/step` with a\n+total effect — retain the value, answer `ok` — until the step stops.\n+\n+- **Every stopping outcome is data, never an exception.** `:ended` is a closed\n+  stream fully read; `:blocked` is an open stream caught up, which is a\n+  snapshot at call time; `:gap` carries the values read before the hole and the\n+  recovery cursor; `:defect` carries the raw answer. The caller decides whether\n+  a partial snapshot is a relation it can query — query has no basis to decide\n+  that for it.\n+- **A cursor never advances past a value the snapshot did not retain**, which\n+  is `step`'s effect-before-commit discipline, and `snapshot` never closes the\n+  handle.\n+\n+`snapshot` stays here until a second consumer needs it, at which point it\n+belongs in `dao.stream.observe` as `drain`.\n \n ## Reading a manifest\n \n-A bounded DaoStream descriptor is opened into a realization.\n+The caller opens a coordinate with `query/open-published!` and releases it with\n+`query/close-published!`; a second close is a no-op.\n \n-- **The implemented published-index DaoStream adapter** opens the declared\n-  DaoJing coordinate, validates the manifest address, fetches only the\n-  manifest, and re-attaches the four covered sets lazily through\n-  `index/restored-indexes` (zero node fetches at open). The EAVT row vector is\n-  deferred behind a delay in the realization and forced only by row consumers.\n-  This gives `q` one uniform Reader+Bound surface.\n+- **`open-published!`** validates the coordinate against\n+  `index/published-index`, opens the declared DaoJing content store, validates\n+  the manifest address, fetches only the manifest, and re-attaches the four\n+  covered sets lazily through `index/restored-indexes` (zero node fetches at\n+  open). The EAVT row vector is deferred behind a delay and forced only by row\n+  consumers. If anything after the store opens throws, the store is closed\n+  before the error propagates. This gives `q` one uniform opened-index value.\n - **The engine consumes those sets directly for selective reads.** A `current`\n   view with `as-of` nil routes `::fact-index` to the restored sets, so\n   3-fixed clauses slice through `subseq-from` and fault only the seek path\n@@ -170,16 +236,23 @@ database interpreters.\n   stream advances its own cursor.\n - **Owner-built, peers compose (implemented)** — each stream's owner indexes\n   its own stream (`dao.space.index/publish-index!`) and persists the segments;\n-  readers consume explicitly supplied descriptors as independent db-values. A\n-  published index descriptor is opened as a logical d5 stream; query composition\n+  readers consume explicitly supplied coordinates as independent db-values. A\n+  reader opens a published index into a d5 relation it owns; query composition\n   stays in the interpreter. Index-once, reuse-by-many, and available\n   when the author is offline — the decentralized analog of Datomic's\n   transactor-built index. Implemented on JVM,\n   ClojureScript, and ClojureDart on `dao.data.btree` (see\n-  `dao.space.index.md`). The stream adapter opens lazily and `current`\n+  `dao.space.index.md`). `open-published!` opens lazily and `current`\n   (as-of nil) selective reads run on the restored trees; eager walks serve\n   `history`, `as-of`, and full scans.\n \n+**Planning never forces the deferred relation.** The clause-cost estimator\n+reads a relation's truthiness without forcing it, and a deferred relation is\n+always truthy, so a multi-clause query plans without draining its source and\n+each clause faults only the slices it touches. This is what makes the lazy\n+route exist for multi-clause queries at all, rather than being a property of\n+single-clause reads.\n+\n ## The `read-datoms` contract\n \n The eager read path is `index/read-datoms`, the function that turns what\n@@ -216,9 +289,8 @@ before building indexes from them — `dao.jing` itself never decodes meaning.\n   that supplies `t = 0` and `m = dao.datom/default-op` (*Source\n   polymorphism*).\n \n-The published-index DaoStream adapter opens lazily (manifest only) and the\n-engine uses the restored sets directly for `current` (as-of nil) selective\n-reads. This eager path remains the contract for `history`, for every `as-of`\n+`open-published!` opens lazily (manifest only) and the engine uses the restored\n+sets directly for `current` (as-of nil) selective reads. This eager path remains the contract for `history`, for every `as-of`\n bound (the clause-level index route has no as-of plumbing; `bound-datoms`\n applies the bound at the relation level), and for any query whose clauses\n genuinely scan. Two observable consequences of the lazy route:\n@@ -233,13 +305,14 @@ genuinely scan. Two observable consequences of the lazy route:\n   resolves current state on the fly, where the eager path scans pre-resolved\n   current-only facts. Same fetches, more per-row work — a cost note, not a\n   correctness difference.\n-- **After close, loudness is the backend's.** A deferred row seq faulted\n-  after `close!` has run reads through the closed store: real backends throw\n-  (`\"missing index segment\"` when a get returns the absent sentinel, or the\n-  backend's own read error); a backend whose close is a no-op (an in-memory\n-  test handle) keeps serving. Never silently empty. Exposure is limited to\n-  manually forcing rows after `close!` — `q`/`match`/`pull` consume and close\n-  inside one dynamic extent.\n+- **After close, loudness is the backend's — and closing is the caller's.** A\n+  deferred row seq faulted after `close-published!` has run reads through the\n+  closed store: real backends throw (`\"missing index segment\"` when a get\n+  returns the absent sentinel, or the backend's own read error); a backend\n+  whose close is a no-op (an in-memory test handle) keeps serving. Never\n+  silently empty. Because `q` opens and closes nothing, the extent in which an\n+  opened index is usable is the caller's to bound: it opens before the query\n+  and closes after it is done with the results.\n - **Planning never forces.** The clause-cost estimator reads the relation's\n   truthiness without forcing it; a deferred relation is always truthy, so\n   multi-clause queries plan without draining the source and each clause\n@@ -374,7 +447,7 @@ force:\n   (e.g. `dao.jing.dht` batching) stays possible but lives entirely inside that\n   backend's own transport.\n - **Coordinate semantics: reference at naming, snapshot at read.** A\n-  published index descriptor names a manifest by content address; each read resolves\n+  published index coordinate names a manifest by content address; each read resolves\n   the immutable value at that address. This is Datomic's `d/db` pattern\n   exactly (a db value is immutable; calling `d/db` again gets a fresher one) —\n   no new mechanism, just \"immutable segments + explicit address.\"\n@@ -390,15 +463,34 @@ force:\n   the observer design, and the tuple space all depend on `dao.stream` staying\n   upstream plumbing and `dao.jing` staying the dumb boundary; unifying them\n   would undo that.\n+- **Bounded realizations are values, not streams.** The DaoStream reader\n+  surface exists so independent cursors can observe one positioned,\n+  append-only, retained sequence *over time*, so a descriptor can name it\n+  across a serialization boundary, and so eviction is reported as a gap. A row\n+  vector computed in full has none of those needs — no second observer, no\n+  future appends, no eviction, no reachability. Dressing one as a reader would\n+  owe a manifest, an exclusion reason for every outcome it cannot produce, and\n+  the conformance harness, and would buy a `next` loop over `nth`. So\n+  relations, views and results are plain tagged values, and `rows` gives a\n+  view's resolved rows to a caller that wants them.\n+- **The relation transport is eliminated.** The v1\n+  `:dao.stream/relation` descriptor carried its tuples *inside itself* and\n+  \"opened\" into a record that read them back by index. A descriptor names a\n+  stream; a descriptor that *is* the data is not reachability data, it is a\n+  value with a transport type stamped on it. `query/relation` returns that\n+  value directly, and a relation value travels through any stream that can\n+  carry data — which is all the transport ever provided.\n \n ## Open items\n \n-- **Published indexed snapshots and K-way merge** — several explicitly scoped manifests\n-  could be exposed as an explicit derived relation without flattening source\n-  identity, then answered by merging N restored B-trees in index order.\n+- **Published indexed snapshots and K-way merge** — several explicitly scoped\n+  manifests could be exposed as an explicit derived relation without flattening\n+  source identity, then answered by merging N restored B-trees in index order.\n+  Passing several opened indexes as separate `:in` values is already supported;\n+  what remains is merging them into one relation.\n - **Generic relation positional indexes** — a positional index orders a\n   relation by explicitly selected tuple positions without assigning meaning to\n-  those positions. The bounded tuples, their exact bound, and their explicitly\n+  those positions. A relation value's tuples together with their explicitly\n   requested positional indexes form an **indexed snapshot**. Arbitrary tuples\n   currently use a relation scan. An explicit\n   datom interpreter automatically supplies the covered indexes for canonical\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.query.md | sed -n '55,190p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.query.md | sed -n '230,330p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.query.md | sed -n '440,520p'; rg -n -i \\\"which bounded streams|published index descriptor|exact bound|result daostream|result stream|q opens|q.*closes|descriptor is opened|reader\\\\+bound|already-opened realization|stream wrapper|local realization|not serializable|by definition not shared\\\" docs/design/dao.space.query.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.query.md | sed -n '55,190p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.query.md | sed -n '230,330p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.query.md | sed -n '440,520p'; rg -n -i \\\"which bounded streams|published index descriptor|exact bound|result daostream|result stream|q opens|q.*closes|descriptor is opened|reader\\\\+bound|already-opened realization|stream wrapper|local realization|not serializable|by definition not shared\\\" docs/design/dao.space.query.md\"","aggregated_output":"    55\t\n    56\t```clojure\n    57\t(index/published-index\n    58\t  {:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/file\n    59\t   :path \"/srv/datom-world/content.log\"}\n    60\t  :segment/sha256-<manifest-hash>)\n    61\t\n    62\t;; The resulting coordinate has this exact data shape. The :dao.stream/*\n    63\t;; keys are dao.space.index's. `open-published!` validates that exact\n    64\t;; shape before opening anything; `q` dispatches on neither key.\n    65\t{:dao.stream/type :dao.space.index/published\n    66\t :dao.stream/bound {:manifest-address :segment/sha256-<manifest-hash>}\n    67\t :dao.stream/comparator :dao.space.index/eavt\n    68\t :content-store {:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/file\n    69\t                 :path \"/srv/datom-world/content.log\"}\n    70\t :manifest-address :segment/sha256-<manifest-hash>}\n    71\t```\n    72\t\n    73\tA query may receive several opened indexes as separate `:in` database values.\n    74\tThe coordinate is itself data and may be transported through a stream, then\n    75\topened by whoever receives it. Source identity is interpreter context and never\n    76\ta tuple slot. It is never inferred from a DaoJing intake stream, and **a bare\n    77\tcontent-store handle carries no source**: it names no manifest, so\n    78\t`q`/`match`/`pull` reject it like any other loose value.\n    79\t\n    80\tTwo kinds of pools must not be confused:\n    81\t\n    82\t- **DaoJing intake pools** are physical ingestion topology — which streams an\n    83\t  observer materializes into content storage.\n    84\t- **Query db-values** are semantic composition — which relation values and\n    85\t  opened indexes a reader names independently in `:in`.\n    86\t\n    87\tThey never need to coincide, and DaoJing never learns the latter. A manifest\n    88\taddress is a content address like any other; it is a snapshot-at-read of an\n    89\timmutable value, not a mutable \"root\" reference, and the query layer treats it\n    90\tas such.\n    91\t\n    92\t## Source polymorphism\n    93\t\n    94\t`dao.space.query/q` accepts database values only as:\n    95\t\n    96\t1. A **relation value** — `(query/relation tuples)` or\n    97\t   `(query/entity-map-relation maps)`.\n    98\t2. A **datom view** over one — `(current src)` / `(history src)`, where `src`\n    99\t   is a relation value, a `snapshot` result, or an opened published index.\n   100\t3. An **opened published index** — `(query/open-published! coordinate)`.\n   101\t\n   102\tRaw maps and vectors are not `q` database inputs, and neither is anything else\n   103\tloose: an unrecognized value throws before evaluation, naming what is accepted.\n   104\tCallers explicitly wrap arbitrary mixed-dimensional tuples with\n   105\t`query/relation` and entity maps with `query/entity-map-relation`.\n   106\t\n   107\tEach database input is one logical relation:\n   108\t\n   109\t- **A published index** — the caller opens the serializable coordinate with\n   110\t  `open-published!`, holds the content-store handle, and releases it with\n   111\t  `close-published!`. `q` borrows it and never closes it. There is no\n   112\t  registry and no dispatch on a transport type: opening a coordinate is\n   113\t  `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` plus a manifest read, a function call the caller\n   114\t  makes, not a stream operation.\n   115\t- **An explicit datom view** — `current` and `history` are semantic view\n   116\t  values interpreted by `q`/`match`/`pull`; their nested source is a relation\n   117\t  value, a `snapshot` result (its relation is taken), or an opened published\n   118\t  index.\n   119\t  `(current source)` resolves a canonical d5 history and projects it to d3;\n   120\t  `(history source)` preserves exact d5. Independent physical sources stay\n   121\t  separate database inputs.\n   122\t- **A wrapped relation** — `(query/relation [[...] ...])` wrapping arbitrary\n   123\t  and mixed arities. Arity never selects an interpretation.\n   124\t- **A wrapped entity-map collection** —\n   125\t  `(query/entity-map-relation [{:db/id e, :work/status :todo, ...} ...])`.\n   126\t  Normalized to datoms first: each `k v` pair becomes an `[e k v]` fact.\n   127\t  Identity is explicit: the read side is pure and never mints an entity id, so\n   128\t  every entity map must carry `:db/id`, and a map without one throws an\n   129\t  informative `ex-info` rather than inventing a tempid.\n   130\t\n   131\t`q` consumes each input exactly as far as evaluation requires, and **opens and\n   132\tcloses nothing** — it is a pure function of its arguments. A `current` view\n   133\t(`as-of` nil) over an opened published index is realized **lazily**: the\n   134\tcovered sets are re-attached as restored B-trees and only the slices the plan\n   135\ttouches are fetched; every other input — history views, `as-of` bounds,\n   136\tin-process relations — is consumed eagerly before returning. It performs\n   137\tarbitrary-dimensional exact positional matching/unification (with explicit `&`\n   138\ttail syntax) and returns a local **result value** carrying the find spec.\n   139\t`query/collect` materializes conventional Datalog shapes from it.\n   140\t\n   141\tRelation values, view values and result values are values, not transports.\n   142\tThey carry no `:dao.stream/type` and no bound: a finite collection computed in\n   143\tfull has no second observer, no future appends and no eviction, so nothing\n   144\tabout the DaoStream reader surface would describe it (see *Decisions*). None of\n   145\tthem carries a reopenable transport identity — a result names nothing another\n   146\truntime could resolve. Their *contents*, being ordinary data, can of course\n   147\ttravel through a stream that carries data; to hand a result on that way,\n   148\tmaterialize it with `query/collect` and wrap the retained tuples explicitly\n   149\twith `query/relation`.\n   150\t\n   151\tSource polymorphism is an ergonomic property of the query *function*, not a\n   152\tsecond medium — a query value is not itself a shared medium, and coordination\n   153\tbetween agents still runs through shared content storage.\n   154\t\n   155\t## Snapshots: the one stream interpreter\n   156\t\n   157\tQuery never reads a live stream during evaluation. A DaoStream v2 reader\n   158\tbecomes a query input only by an explicit snapshot, and `snapshot` is the only\n   159\tfunction in this namespace that requires `dao.stream`:\n   160\t\n   161\t```clojure\n   162\t(query/snapshot handle)\n   163\t;; => {:relation {:dao.space.query/relation [v …]}\n   164\t;;     :status   :ended | :blocked | :gap | :defect\n   165\t;;     :cursor   c            ; the cursor reached; on :gap the last retained\n   166\t;;     :recovery c'           ; :gap only\n   167\t;;     :read     raw}         ; :defect only\n   168\t```\n   169\t\n   170\tIt mints at `:dao.stream/oldest` and loops `dao.stream.observe/step` with a\n   171\ttotal effect — retain the value, answer `ok` — until the step stops.\n   172\t\n   173\t- **Every stopping outcome is data, never an exception.** `:ended` is a closed\n   174\t  stream fully read; `:blocked` is an open stream caught up, which is a\n   175\t  snapshot at call time; `:gap` carries the values read before the hole and the\n   176\t  recovery cursor; `:defect` carries the raw answer. The caller decides whether\n   177\t  a partial snapshot is a relation it can query — query has no basis to decide\n   178\t  that for it.\n   179\t- **A cursor never advances past a value the snapshot did not retain**, which\n   180\t  is `step`'s effect-before-commit discipline, and `snapshot` never closes the\n   181\t  handle.\n   182\t\n   183\t`snapshot` stays here until a second consumer needs it, at which point it\n   184\tbelongs in `dao.stream.observe` as `drain`.\n   185\t\n   186\t## Reading a manifest\n   187\t\n   188\tThe caller opens a coordinate with `query/open-published!` and releases it with\n   189\t`query/close-published!`; a second close is a no-op.\n   190\t\n   230\t- **Rebuild per query** — each read folds the source's datoms into a fresh\n   231\t  index and discards it. Simple; O(total datoms) per read. The permanent\n   232\t  fallback for small sources and `as-of` reads.\n   233\t- **Incremental index** — a long-lived reader keeps a cursor per source\n   234\t  stream and folds only new frames as they arrive (Datomic-peer style). More\n   235\t  machinery; amortized reads. Still no transactor and no global clock — each\n   236\t  stream advances its own cursor.\n   237\t- **Owner-built, peers compose (implemented)** — each stream's owner indexes\n   238\t  its own stream (`dao.space.index/publish-index!`) and persists the segments;\n   239\t  readers consume explicitly supplied coordinates as independent db-values. A\n   240\t  reader opens a published index into a d5 relation it owns; query composition\n   241\t  stays in the interpreter. Index-once, reuse-by-many, and available\n   242\t  when the author is offline — the decentralized analog of Datomic's\n   243\t  transactor-built index. Implemented on JVM,\n   244\t  ClojureScript, and ClojureDart on `dao.data.btree` (see\n   245\t  `dao.space.index.md`). `open-published!` opens lazily and `current`\n   246\t  (as-of nil) selective reads run on the restored trees; eager walks serve\n   247\t  `history`, `as-of`, and full scans.\n   248\t\n   249\t**Planning never forces the deferred relation.** The clause-cost estimator\n   250\treads a relation's truthiness without forcing it, and a deferred relation is\n   251\talways truthy, so a multi-clause query plans without draining its source and\n   252\teach clause faults only the slices it touches. This is what makes the lazy\n   253\troute exist for multi-clause queries at all, rather than being a property of\n   254\tsingle-clause reads.\n   255\t\n   256\t## The `read-datoms` contract\n   257\t\n   258\tThe eager read path is `index/read-datoms`, the function that turns what\n   259\t`jing/get` returns into the `[e a v t m]` vectors the index is built from:\n   260\t\n   261\t```clojure\n   262\t(defn read-datoms [content-store manifest-address]\n   263\t  (let [manifest (read-manifest content-store manifest-address)]\n   264\t    (vec (walk-index-datoms content-store (:eavt (:indexes manifest))))))\n   265\t```\n   266\t\n   267\tIt is a pure data-structure walk over whatever `jing/get` returns — it never\n   268\ttouches bytes or calls `edn/read-string` itself. `jing/get`'s contract is\n   269\t\"returns the stored opaque value at a strict segment address, already\n   270\tdecoded.\" Whether that value arrived via an EDN round-trip (`dao.jing.file`), a\n   271\tplain in-memory reference (`dao.jing.mem`), an RPC (`dao.jing.remote`), or a\n   272\tDHT fetch\n   273\t(`dao.jing.dht`) is backend-internal and invisible here. This is exactly the\n   274\tseam Datomic's Peer occupies internally when it decodes storage's index blobs\n   275\tbefore building indexes from them — `dao.jing` itself never decodes meaning.\n   276\t\n   277\t**Current contract:**\n   278\t\n   279\t- **Input.** A single `jing/get` of the manifest address, returning an\n   280\t  already-decoded, validated manifest.\n   281\t- **Extract.** `walk-index-datoms` reads the `:eavt` node graph eagerly, one\n   282\t  `jing/get` per node (plain EDN leaf/branch blobs), and returns the flat\n   283\t  datoms seq in index order. A nil address (an empty index) walks to ().\n   284\t- **Canonical d5 only.** Local streams and covered indexes store exactly\n   285\t  `[e a v t m]`. `read-datoms` preserves those vectors. Source scope is held\n   286\t  by the query interpreter and never stamped into a tuple.\n   287\t- **Tuple preservation.** `read-datoms` returns stored vectors unchanged.\n   288\t  Entity-map source normalization is a separate query-boundary convenience\n   289\t  that supplies `t = 0` and `m = dao.datom/default-op` (*Source\n   290\t  polymorphism*).\n   291\t\n   292\t`open-published!` opens lazily (manifest only) and the engine uses the restored\n   293\tsets directly for `current` (as-of nil) selective reads. This eager path remains the contract for `history`, for every `as-of`\n   294\tbound (the clause-level index route has no as-of plumbing; `bound-datoms`\n   295\tapplies the bound at the relation level), and for any query whose clauses\n   296\tgenuinely scan. Two observable consequences of the lazy route:\n   297\t\n   298\t- **t/m provenance.** The eager in-memory fact index rebuilds rows with\n   299\t  synthetic `t = 0` and `m = dao.datom/default-op`; the restored sets carry the\n   300\t  real `t`/`m`. The public `datoms` selector exposes the difference. Clause\n   301\t  evaluation projects `[e a v]`, so `q`/`pull`/`get-else` results are\n   302\t  unaffected; parity tests pin the `[e a v]` projections.\n   303\t- **Unselective scans do more CPU work lazily.** A clause with no bound\n   304\t  e/a/v walks the full d5 history through the `(seq (:eavt idx))` fallback and\n   305\t  resolves current state on the fly, where the eager path scans pre-resolved\n   306\t  current-only facts. Same fetches, more per-row work — a cost note, not a\n   307\t  correctness difference.\n   308\t- **After close, loudness is the backend's — and closing is the caller's.** A\n   309\t  deferred row seq faulted after `close-published!` has run reads through the\n   310\t  closed store: real backends throw (`\"missing index segment\"` when a get\n   311\t  returns the absent sentinel, or the backend's own read error); a backend\n   312\t  whose close is a no-op (an in-memory test handle) keeps serving. Never\n   313\t  silently empty. Because `q` opens and closes nothing, the extent in which an\n   314\t  opened index is usable is the caller's to bound: it opens before the query\n   315\t  and closes after it is done with the results.\n   316\t- **Planning never forces.** The clause-cost estimator reads the relation's\n   317\t  truthiness without forcing it; a deferred relation is always truthy, so\n   318\t  multi-clause queries plan without draining the source and each clause\n   319\t  faults only the slices it touches (pinned by the multi-clause budget test).\n   320\t\n   321\t## Current-state resolution\n   322\t\n   323\tThe log is append-only, so a retraction is another d5 datom. Interpretation\n   324\tis explicit:\n   325\t\n   326\t- `(current source)` bounds by `as-of`, resolves the greatest `(t,m)` for\n   327\t  each local `[e a v]`, removes retractions, and projects to d3.\n   328\t- `(history source)` exposes the exact d5 relation, optionally bounded by\n   329\t  `as-of`.\n   330\t- A bare vector relation has no temporal semantics, even when every row has\n   440\t  needs is built on `dao.jing/materialize!` and `dao.jing/get` over strict\n   441\t  segment addresses (see `dao.space.index.md`). Immutable B-tree nodes are\n   442\t  just more content-addressed blobs; lazy traversal, when an interpreter uses\n   443\t  `restored-indexes`, is a **reader-side** property — the tree pulls a node only when traversal reaches it, and storage\n   444\t  never scans or seeks, it just answers `get`. Any \"storage-side\n   445\t  materialization\" would mean a backend *computing* something, which collides\n   446\t  head-on with storage-never-interprets. A backend-private network shortcut\n   447\t  (e.g. `dao.jing.dht` batching) stays possible but lives entirely inside that\n   448\t  backend's own transport.\n   449\t- **Coordinate semantics: reference at naming, snapshot at read.** A\n   450\t  published index coordinate names a manifest by content address; each read resolves\n   451\t  the immutable value at that address. This is Datomic's `d/db` pattern\n   452\t  exactly (a db value is immutable; calling `d/db` again gets a fresher one) —\n   453\t  no new mechanism, just \"immutable segments + explicit address.\"\n   454\t- **Owner-built, peers-merge is the target (implemented).** Incremental\n   455\t  indexing is the degenerate case where owner and reader coincide.\n   456\t  Rebuild-per-query stays the permanent fallback for small sources or when no\n   457\t  persisted manifest exists yet.\n   458\t- **Freshness: explicit, monotone lag.** A reader's merged view reflects\n   459\t  whatever manifest each source names *at fold time* — never live, never\n   460\t  blocking.\n   461\t- **`dao.stream`/`dao.jing` unification: ruled out.** The source stays a\n   462\t  convention layered over the dumb content store. The DHT division of labor,\n   463\t  the observer design, and the tuple space all depend on `dao.stream` staying\n   464\t  upstream plumbing and `dao.jing` staying the dumb boundary; unifying them\n   465\t  would undo that.\n   466\t- **Bounded realizations are values, not streams.** The DaoStream reader\n   467\t  surface exists so independent cursors can observe one positioned,\n   468\t  append-only, retained sequence *over time*, so a descriptor can name it\n   469\t  across a serialization boundary, and so eviction is reported as a gap. A row\n   470\t  vector computed in full has none of those needs — no second observer, no\n   471\t  future appends, no eviction, no reachability. Dressing one as a reader would\n   472\t  owe a manifest, an exclusion reason for every outcome it cannot produce, and\n   473\t  the conformance harness,